WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.940
As we get started here today, I think I'm going to invite you to open up to 1 John chapter 1.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:11.280
As we get there, today's Sunday celebration is going to be a bit different.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.800
Some of you are aware of that already because you maybe received an email as a member of our church or heard about it.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:22.160
But we're going to be dealing with something in our church's history that's really difficult to talk about.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.180
I just want to acknowledge that up front today.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.520
If you're visiting us for the first time today, well, you might hear what we shared today and decide,

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.780
man, I'm never coming back. Or you may hear what we shared today and say, that's the kind of church

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:40.780
that I want to be part of. And so as we get into this this morning, I just want to acknowledge like

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:45.620
as a broader thing going on in our society, I think there's been a lot of attention given

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:51.560
in recent years to the dysfunction of leadership in the church. There are a lot of ways that

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:57.220
that's very visible and very prominent in our culture and society today. Some of you may be

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:02.420
familiar with, or maybe you've watched, I believe it's a Netflix series called Death of a Pastor's

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:08.400
Wife that came out this year. It's a very sad story, a story that involves a lot of manipulation

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:16.260
and coercion and control, ultimately leading to the death of this pastor's wife. Maybe a few years

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:20.680
ago, you watched something called The Secrets of Hillsong. I think it was released by Hulu.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:26.900
It told a lot about some different sexual misconduct of pastors within this group of

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:32.440
church is and how the church even tried to cover those things up. Or maybe even a couple years

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:38.400
before that, you listen to a podcast called The Rise and Fall of Mars Hill. It talks a lot about

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:44.540
when churches choose charisma in leadership over character and what can happen when abusive

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:51.160
leadership and financial impropriety bring down the community of a church. When we look at all

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:57.720
these things, and I could give a long list of more from people from different traditions and

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:02.940
backgrounds. But in all of this, it seems like more than ever before, famous and quote-unquote

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:10.220
successful pastors are falling from grace. And not just the misbehavior on the part of some of

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:16.080
these pastors, but I think people have come to recognize a pattern from churches and ministries

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:25.180
to minimize, to avoid directly dealing with, to cover up bad behavior from their leaders,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:32.740
especially if that person is gifted or successful or charismatic. People have come to almost expect

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:39.280
that that would be the case. Now, I should also acknowledge, I don't think that businesses like

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:45.620
Netflix or Hulu are very pure in their motives for bringing these things to light. I think they

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:50.620
know what will get views. And I think there are also plenty of people who are delighted to see

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:57.640
Christian leaders fall and ministries be affected. But at the same time, I believe that our God who

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:05.300
redeems all things is purifying his church. And that's a good thing, even if it's painful to go

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:11.000
through. Today I have the difficult but important task of sharing an update with you about our

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:17.260
former pastor's misconduct. In February of this year, some of you were there at a congregational

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:23.240
meeting when I shared that there was a serious accusation that had been brought up regarding

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:29.240
our former pastor, Seth Kim. We shared very generally at the time, but we want to bring

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:34.960
full clarity and transparency to that situation today. I'll also highlight as we start that this

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:40.880
is a complex situation, and I want to kind of give some context around it to just help aid in

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:46.000
our understanding of how everything played out, especially for those of you who may be unfamiliar.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:52.460
To give you a little history, Seth Kim was the founding and lead pastor of HMCC of Ann Arbor

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:59.820
from 1996 to 2015. After that, he led a church plant team to start HMCC of Hong Kong in 2015.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:04.520
That's when I became the lead pastor of HMCC of Ann Arbor here that January.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:10.500
Some of you also know that all the HMCC churches chose to part ways organizationally

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:16.760
in 2024. After that, most of the churches remained independent, but HMCC of Austin and Hong Kong

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:22.440
decided to form a new organization together with one another. I want to be clear today that I'm not

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:26.840
speaking on behalf of any of the other HMCC churches or pastors or those who used to be

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:33.380
called HMCC, as some have changed their names. But as we dig into this, some of you, especially

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:38.660
here who are new to our church, and most of you are new to our church in the last five, six years

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:44.500
or so, you know, a lot of new people after COVID. Some of you may be wondering, who is this person?

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:51.340
The last time that Seth visited our church here was in 2017, and many of you have actually never

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:57.040
met him. Maybe you've never heard of him until today, but for a lot of people who've been with

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.280
our church here over the years, and even people who have been with us and are now all over the

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:08.320
country, all over the world, they still associate Seth with HMCC of Ann Arbor. And so for myself

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:14.140
and the elders, we felt like we had a responsibility to directly address all of this. Before I get into

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:19.300
the specifics, I will also mention that after the celebration today at 1230, we're going to be

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:24.320
having a Q&A session that's open to anyone here who wants to come. This is specifically for our

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:29.180
church community. I recognize that today there may be more people than usual watching online or

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:33.920
listening to this sermon later. This Q&A session is time for us as a church family to be together.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.220
It's not going to be live-streamed or shared in any way.

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:39.360
But we want to create space for those of you who have questions.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:42.340
I also want to mention, maybe some of you can't make that time today,

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:45.280
you are very welcome to contact any of the elders

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.440
with any questions or concerns that you have.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:51.820
We want to be open and transparent in every way with you

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:57.060
and try as best as we can to address any of your questions or concerns along with that.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:00.220
Also, I'll mention that today's topic is pretty heavy,

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:03.860
and we will have just some of our prayer team available at the end of the celebration.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:08.840
For those of you who feel like you need prayer personally, going through some things, we want to be there to support you.

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:11.960
I'm going to read a written statement to you to start.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:17.980
The content of this statement is going to be posted to our church's website after the celebration today.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:20.340
So you don't need to look for it right now. It's not there yet.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:23.800
After the celebration, it will be posted on our church's website.

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:25.760
I'm going to go ahead and read this for us.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:32.340
This statement is written on behalf of HMCC of Ann Arbor and the anonymous victims referenced below.

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.720
Dear HMCC members, alumni, and friends,

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:41.000
we are publishing this statement to bring the misconduct of our former pastor, Seth S. Kim, into the light.

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:46.180
On December 30, 2025, HMCC of Ann Arbor received a letter from a law firm

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:50.240
representing an individual who accused Seth of adult clergy sexual misconduct.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:53.060
We will refer to this individual as Survivor A.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:57.240
Specifically, the letter detailed how Seth used his position of spiritual authority

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.320
to groom and manipulate Survivor A,

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:03.000
arranging private meetings under the guise of discipleship

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:07.740
to initiate unwanted sexual conduct in Asia over a long period of time.

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:09.780
As her pastor and spiritual leader,

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:12.660
Seth held a position of significant authority and trust.

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:14.780
This was not merely a moral failure,

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:17.740
but a grave misuse of entrusted spiritual authority

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:19.520
and a breach of ministerial ethics.

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:23.520
Although HMCC of Ann Arbor had previously disassociated with Seth,

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:26.800
we believed it was our responsibility to act on this information

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:29.740
and notify those in positions to immediately protect others.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:32.880
We therefore contacted the pastor of HMCC of Austin

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.380
to inform him of the accusations

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:37.400
and asked him to have Seth step down from his role

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:39.520
as pastor of HMCC of Hong Kong,

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:41.900
which he did in early January, 2026.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:44.220
We have also confirmed that Seth has resigned

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:45.560
from roles of ministry leadership

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:47.640
that he held with several other organizations.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:49.740
In the following weeks and months,

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:52.360
we carefully followed up on the information we received,

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:55.160
sought legal counsel, and went through a formal mediation.

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:59.720
During this process, our desire was to honor God, protect the survivor's identity,

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:04.220
hold Seth accountable for his actions, and work toward an appropriate public response.

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:07.840
At mediation, we agreed to collaborate on this public statement

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:11.320
to ensure that Seth would no longer be able to harm those under his leadership.

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:15.860
While Seth deliberately concealed his misconduct through secret communication,

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.480
spiritual manipulation, isolation, and fear,

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:23.180
we recognized that survivors rightly expect churches to be places of safety and protection.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:25.920
Seth intentionally deceived those around him,

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:29.120
but we grieve that our community was not able to uncover what was happening sooner.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:34.420
HMCC of Ann Arbor and other HMCC churches had policies, accountability practices,

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:36.720
and reporting pathways in place.

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:40.220
However, we recognize that policies alone cannot prevent misconduct

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:43.540
and that we must continually build a culture that encourages transparency,

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:45.920
makes it safe for people to raise concerns,

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:49.500
and responds with compassion, diligence, and accountability when they do.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:51.660
We are committed to learning from these events

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:54.340
and evaluating and strengthening our accountability practices.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:58.360
Survivor A will remain publicly anonymous, but is known to us.

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:02.700
In recent months, we received a letter from legal counsel representing a second survivor.

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:08.280
Survivor B described remarkably similar experiences of grooming, manipulation, and sexual misconduct

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:11.380
taking place in Ann Arbor while Seth was serving as lead pastor.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:16.860
Survivor B has chosen to remain anonymous to us, but we take her report seriously.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:21.620
On the basis of these two survivors' reports of misconduct occurring over many years,

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.860
as well as supporting evidence we have seen.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:26.680
We believe these accusations against Seth are true.

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:29.040
Counselors and advocates for victims

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:31.900
describe the power dynamics within clergy sexual misconduct

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:33.920
as making genuine consent impossible.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:36.100
Both survivors want to make clear

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:38.860
that they did not initiate and did not want this behavior.

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:40.700
We are grateful to these survivors

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:42.400
for the courage it took to come forward.

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:44.960
Speaking the truth is an act of courage and integrity

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:46.280
that helps protect others.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:48.580
We apologize to both survivors

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:51.040
for the mistreatment they suffered at Seth's hands.

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:56.820
According to 1 Peter 5, 1-5, 1 Timothy 3, 1-7, and 5, 19-22,

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:04.720
James 3, 1, 1 Thessalonians 4, 3-6, 1 Corinthians 5, 9-13, and Titus 1, 6-9,

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:08.600
we believe that Seth has permanently disqualified himself from pastoral ministry.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:13.360
Because he used pastoral authority to exploit and manipulate someone under his spiritual care,

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:18.900
he should not be entrusted with any position of Christian leadership, spiritual authority, or mentorship.

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:20.940
To our knowledge at the present time,

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:23.520
Seth has not cooperated with attempts to hold him accountable,

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:25.500
and we therefore advise all Christians

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:28.600
to apply the principles of Matthew 18, 15 to 17

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:30.300
in any interactions with him.

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:33.060
For many of us who have been part of HMCC churches

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:35.820
over the years, this is difficult news to hear and process.

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:38.380
We recognize that misconduct by a pastor

00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:40.240
can profoundly damage a person's faith,

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:42.700
trust, relationships, and sense of safety,

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:45.060
and that survivors may experience renewed grief,

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:48.500
anger, fear, or confusion when reading this statement.

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:52.960
If this brings up painful experiences for you, pastoral support and referrals to professional

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:57.280
counseling are available. You are welcome to contact any of the pastors and elders if you have

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:02.160
questions, concerns, or simply need someone to listen and pray with you or to help connect you

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:06.420
with additional support. We recognize that misconduct of this nature is rarely isolated.

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:11.380
If anyone has experienced inappropriate conduct or has relevant information, we encourage you to

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:15.920
contact the elders. We pray for healing for the survivors and those affected by these events

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:18.020
and genuine repentance and restoration.

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:19.940
And there are a couple of links

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:21.680
to some additional resources at the end.

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:27.780
I want to just pause and recognize

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:28.900
that this is a lot to process.

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:40.350
When we first learned and shared

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:41.870
about these accusations in February,

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:45.270
we knew only of the misconduct taking place in Asia.

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:48.830
But as I shared, Survivor B has indicated

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:51.270
that with her this happened in Ann Arbor here.

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:55.090
I want to start by just saying

00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:57.810
that the elders express our deep and genuine gratitude

00:11:57.810 --> 00:11:59.530
to these two women for coming forward.

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:05.470
As hard as it was, you did the right thing, and we're genuinely thankful that you decided to

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:11.950
contact us. I also want to highlight that this is not only a statement from myself and HMCC of Ann

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:19.110
Arbor, that this has been collaborated together with the two survivors. It's taken a long time to

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:23.850
get to this point because we've been communicating back and forth between us and all the lawyers that

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:28.950
are involved, and that's taken a very long time, but we wanted to go through the hard work to make

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:34.450
sure that we got this right. I'll also mention that a lot of extra details related to times,

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:39.350
places, and events have not been included in this statement in order to prevent confidentiality,

00:12:39.490 --> 00:12:42.830
and those things will not be shared because we deeply care about that for these sisters.

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:50.690
Where do we go from here? How do we process all of this both personally and even together as the

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:55.710
family of the church? And I think in any situation where we want to turn is to the Word of God.

00:12:56.330 --> 00:13:00.030
And so I'd like to read for us today from 1 John 1, verses 5 through 10.

00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:01.590
I'll read this passage for us.

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:06.550
This is the message we have heard from him and proclaim to you,

00:13:06.950 --> 00:13:10.110
that God is light and in him is no darkness at all.

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:13.450
If we say we have fellowship with him while we walk in darkness,

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:15.810
we lie and do not practice the truth.

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:18.750
But if we walk in the light as he is in the light,

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:20.450
we have fellowship with one another,

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:23.730
and the blood of Jesus his Son cleanses us from all sin.

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:26.810
If we say we have no sin, we deceive ourselves.

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:28.190
and the truth is not in us.

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:29.790
If we confess our sins,

00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:30.950
he is faithful and just

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:32.090
to forgive us our sins

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:32.890
and to cleanse us

00:13:32.890 --> 00:13:34.190
from all unrighteousness.

00:13:34.690 --> 00:13:36.170
If we say we have not sinned,

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:37.650
we make him a liar,

00:13:37.910 --> 00:13:39.390
and his word is not in us.

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:40.010
Amen.

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:41.410
Pray for us one more time.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:45.440
Lord Jesus,

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:46.760
we invite you into this time

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:48.140
we're spending together here today.

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:49.920
And I pray, Lord,

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:51.000
for your guidance,

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:52.520
for your wisdom,

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:54.120
for your counsel,

00:13:54.380 --> 00:13:55.080
for your comfort,

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:56.720
for all of us here,

00:13:57.760 --> 00:13:58.440
for all of us,

00:13:58.480 --> 00:13:59.680
even from many different places

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:01.260
that may join or hear this online,

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:03.800
for all who have been affected

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:05.940
by these events that we're talking about today,

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:07.400
we invite you, Lord,

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:09.000
to come and minister to us,

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:12.020
to guide us, to lead us,

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:13.280
to speak to our hearts,

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:15.660
especially to speak through your holy word

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:17.220
that is a gift to us,

00:14:17.420 --> 00:14:19.040
especially in times of need.

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:21.660
We pray, Lord, that you would speak to us today,

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:24.020
that you help us both individually and together

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:25.220
as the family of the church

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:26.860
to process these events,

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:28.940
to seek you, Jesus,

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:34.580
us to seek how to live out the gospel together as a spiritual family in response to all of this.

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:40.720
So bless us and be with us today, Lord. May your name be glorified, Jesus, and we ask that your will

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:48.720
will be done among us. In Jesus' name we pray. Amen. Amen. So once again, as Christians, what do

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:55.800
we do when serious sin happens? And what do we do when the person who has perpetrated that sin

00:14:55.800 --> 00:15:03.120
is a pastor who many people look up have looked up to trusted followed what do we do with that

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:08.280
as I read this passage to you today I'm going to share some of my own reflections through the passage

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:14.240
not as much as like a point by point uh you know teaching of it but more so to reflect through

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:19.720
these verses and think about how we can process these events today and the place that I think we

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:27.560
must start, that's necessary to start, is to say that we must trust God's character. In these verses

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:34.520
we are told God is light and in him is no darkness at all. We find out from this that God's holiness

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:41.380
is not just a theological concept that we sort of think about and imagine, but this is exactly

00:15:41.380 --> 00:15:48.360
what we need to focus on when serious sin happens. For some of us, I think our first reaction to

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:54.760
hearing news like I've shared with you today, is to doubt or at least question God. You may think

00:15:54.760 --> 00:16:01.600
things like, God, how can you be good when things like this not only happen but happen in the church

00:16:01.600 --> 00:16:08.920
by a Christian leader? It makes us ask those questions. Most of you know we've been going

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:13.860
through Ephesians as a church, and one of the themes we've talked about is trusting God. And

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:19.940
We've also talked about one of the realities, that because people aren't machines, aren't

00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:24.780
just obeying their programming, but can make real decisions and can choose not to obey

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:29.880
God, that there's a lot of pain and brokenness in the world, as we're talking about today.

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:36.280
We trust God because somehow, over all of the human rebellion, over all the human sin,

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:40.340
God is still pure and holy and true and good.

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:48.460
A question I think many of us may need to wrestle more with today is, am I trusting God or am I

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:55.240
trusting a person when it comes to living out my Christian life? I've had to do a lot of internal

00:16:55.240 --> 00:17:00.840
wrestling with the Lord in my own heart over this. It's been going on for many months and it's been a

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:05.960
pretty difficult season for myself and many people. One of the things that I've realized as I've

00:17:05.960 --> 00:17:12.520
reflected and kind of tried to ask God to point out what's going on inside of myself is that I

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:19.340
really want people to trust me. Maybe a better way of saying that is I want to be worthy of people's

00:17:19.340 --> 00:17:27.260
trust. I want that. But God has challenged me on this. Because on the one hand, I think it is good

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:34.260
for people to be able to trust pastors, elders, other church leaders. And it's good. And it's

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:39.520
taught in the scriptures that we should have trustworthy leadership in the church to follow.

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:48.000
But on the other hand, following Jesus, being part of the church, serving God, all the other things we

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:55.640
do in Christian life must primarily be about trusting God, not trusting a person. That is so

00:17:55.640 --> 00:18:00.820
important. And I think as I've gone through this and gone through this with many people now over

00:18:00.820 --> 00:18:05.660
the past months, I realized that the deepest struggles with events like these actually come

00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:10.800
from putting a person in the place of God and living out our Christian life in a way that

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:18.700
focuses on trusting a person, a leader, rather than primarily trusting God. Once again, God is

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:24.480
light and in him is no darkness at all. I want to point out this can only be said about God.

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:30.320
It can't be said about any other person, about me or anyone else.

00:18:30.980 --> 00:18:36.920
We can't say, Pete is light and in him is no darkness at all.

00:18:37.780 --> 00:18:38.860
We can't say that.

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:46.400
And so no person should become the primary focus of our trust and followership and faith and Christian life.

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:49.380
We must put that purely in God.

00:18:49.380 --> 00:18:56.080
And so we must start this whole journey by trusting God to be the shepherd who leads us through these kind of dark valleys.

00:18:56.900 --> 00:19:01.620
Now, as we start there, trusting God and his character to shepherd us through this,

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:06.220
then we need to get into some of the very important but difficult things.

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:11.040
I'd like to say that where we probably should start is that we should call sin, sin.

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:13.640
We should call sin, sin.

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:21.060
I think that Christians should be the most honest and direct about sin compared to anybody else.

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:28.260
As it says in verse 8 and 10 here, if we say we have no sin, we deceive ourselves.

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:31.820
If we say we have not sinned, we make God a liar.

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:38.260
This means that one thing we should not do, cannot do as Christians and as the church,

00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:39.560
is we should not deny.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:41.240
We should not cover up.

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:42.740
We should not blame the victim.

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:47.800
We should not justify or minimize anything that has happened to try to save face.

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:49.160
We should not do any of that.

00:19:49.480 --> 00:19:56.000
And today we should be able to say plainly that what Seth did was a betrayal to everyone in his life.

00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:01.120
From those who were closest to him to even those who only knew him from a distance.

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:05.960
I also want to say that we need to look a little bit higher than that too.

00:20:07.020 --> 00:20:09.260
This sin was not only against other people.

00:20:09.380 --> 00:20:12.020
It was first and foremost against God.

00:20:12.740 --> 00:20:18.340
All sin is first against our holy God, who is light and righteousness and justice.

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:24.920
And when we see that, when we look at that, we must not do anything to try to avoid the plain

00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:33.100
truth of it. Now, when we call sin, sin, we also need to not just take it as just any other random

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:37.980
event, but I think it's really important that we actually grieve the damage done by it.

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:41.380
That we grieve the damage that's done by it.

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:45.520
I think the biblical practice of mourning and grieving over sin

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:47.400
doesn't get a lot of attention today.

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:49.900
It's not fun or pleasant to talk about,

00:20:49.900 --> 00:20:54.280
but I think it is a needed response when we see the damage that sin does,

00:20:54.420 --> 00:20:56.920
and we shouldn't simply move on from that.

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:02.880
I was unsure whether to share this personally today about my family,

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:05.620
but I think I will because I think it'll be helpful.

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:09.520
Some of you know my family has two cats, Sophie and Izzy.

00:21:10.380 --> 00:21:13.140
And sadly, Izzy actually passed away yesterday.

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:22.580
She was only four years old, but she developed severe kidney failure.

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:25.280
And by the time we found out, there wasn't anything we could do.

00:21:26.580 --> 00:21:30.900
And so yesterday morning, Nicholas and I came home from our youth group lock-in.

00:21:31.780 --> 00:21:33.880
Gina had been with Izzy all night long.

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:37.900
And we spent the morning crying together.

00:21:38.080 --> 00:21:41.620
talking to Izzy, telling her how much we loved her,

00:21:42.660 --> 00:21:45.140
sharing good memories about her,

00:21:45.800 --> 00:21:49.120
and trying to make her passing as comfortable as possible.

00:21:51.100 --> 00:21:52.780
It was important that we do that.

00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:56.660
If we didn't do that, I mean, I know not all of you here,

00:21:56.660 --> 00:21:59.460
maybe animal people or cat people, maybe you don't like cats.

00:21:59.700 --> 00:22:00.000
We do.

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:04.960
But it was important that we did that because if we didn't do that,

00:22:05.020 --> 00:22:06.960
it would be as if she didn't mean something.

00:22:07.800 --> 00:22:10.500
It would be as if we weren't losing something, which we were.

00:22:11.460 --> 00:22:16.940
I share that because in these moments when serious sin happens,

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:20.500
we can't even just kind of make an announcement about it, just move on.

00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:25.420
It's important that we actually be with the Lord and be with one another

00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:28.600
and grieve that because something real has been lost.

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:33.000
Psalm 119 verse 136 says,

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:41.300
my eyes shed streams of tears because people do not keep your law. Do our hearts break and do we

00:22:41.300 --> 00:22:49.840
genuinely grieve when sin happens and brings great damage to people, to the world around us? I think

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:53.860
it's so important as we go through all of this. First, I think it's important that we grieve those

00:22:53.860 --> 00:23:00.240
who are directly impacted by these events. I think we should grieve for these survivors who suffered

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:02.260
this sexual misconduct and all the struggle

00:23:02.260 --> 00:23:04.180
that came with it that they did not

00:23:04.180 --> 00:23:06.160
deserve. We should grieve

00:23:06.160 --> 00:23:07.580
that for them, and we do.

00:23:08.620 --> 00:23:09.660
We should grieve, and

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:12.320
I'll just mention very briefly for Seth's

00:23:12.320 --> 00:23:14.080
family, we grieve

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:15.580
what they must be going through,

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:17.160
and we're praying for you.

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:20.180
We should grieve for those

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:22.080
who have been under Seth's spiritual leadership

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:24.200
over the years. Some of you

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:26.260
here today in this room, and probably some

00:23:26.260 --> 00:23:28.200
of you watching this online, have been

00:23:28.200 --> 00:23:30.220
part of our church family for many years.

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:33.200
and some of you have served directly with Seth

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:34.140
or under his leadership

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:36.820
and hearing this news can be really disillusioning

00:23:36.820 --> 00:23:37.680
and discouraging.

00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:41.220
I shared this before and I'll say again,

00:23:41.600 --> 00:23:43.280
personally, this impacts me a lot.

00:23:44.140 --> 00:23:45.920
Seth was the person who baptized me.

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:47.740
He mentored me.

00:23:48.400 --> 00:23:49.380
He ordained me.

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:51.320
He officiated my wedding

00:23:51.320 --> 00:23:53.900
and as I grew up in this church,

00:23:53.980 --> 00:23:56.360
this is my only church home I've ever known,

00:23:56.740 --> 00:23:58.360
as I grew up, he became not only a mentor

00:23:58.360 --> 00:24:00.140
but someone I would call a friend and a brother.

00:24:02.460 --> 00:24:04.180
Many of us feel this very deeply.

00:24:05.880 --> 00:24:08.800
Along with this grieving comes a hard realization

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:12.700
that God can and does use sinful people.

00:24:14.160 --> 00:24:15.980
In fact, when we stop and think about it,

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:18.560
that's the only option, really.

00:24:20.300 --> 00:24:22.280
If God doesn't use sinful people,

00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:24.840
well, then he can't use me and he can't use any of us here.

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:36.140
I should also say that the fact that God has used someone in ministry

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:39.080
does not validate or cover over that person's sins.

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:43.740
And I want to give an encouragement to all of us here,

00:24:43.860 --> 00:24:45.960
especially many of you who are very invested

00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:47.000
in our church community,

00:24:47.360 --> 00:24:48.480
whether that was in the past

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:49.740
and you're listening online today

00:24:49.740 --> 00:24:51.560
or whether it's those of you here in this room.

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:53.840
An encouragement I want to give to all of you

00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:55.360
is that what we do together,

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:59.300
the prayer, the service, the worship,

00:24:59.540 --> 00:25:01.440
the generosity, the community,

00:25:01.660 --> 00:25:03.560
the early mornings, the late nights,

00:25:03.880 --> 00:25:06.380
everything that gets poured into all of this

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:08.320
that we're trying to live out as a spiritual family

00:25:08.320 --> 00:25:11.580
are not and never can have been for a person.

00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:13.320
They must be for God.

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:17.140
That's something I want all of you to hold on to,

00:25:17.220 --> 00:25:19.100
and that's something I've had to hold on to

00:25:19.100 --> 00:25:21.120
in going through all of this.

00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:24.780
So we grieve for those directly impacted by these events.

00:25:25.080 --> 00:25:26.960
We also grieve for the body of Christ

00:25:26.960 --> 00:25:28.360
and for our witness to the world.

00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:31.340
For some people, this is just one more reason

00:25:31.340 --> 00:25:34.500
not to trust the church and not to believe in the gospel,

00:25:35.100 --> 00:25:36.360
and we should grieve over that.

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:40.340
Many of you here may be newer to our church family,

00:25:40.560 --> 00:25:42.320
and even as I share this news,

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:44.700
and maybe you don't know Seth personally still,

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:47.560
this causes uncertainty, a doubt,

00:25:47.820 --> 00:25:49.620
and doubt about, man, what are we doing here?

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:53.120
We should grieve over that.

00:25:54.380 --> 00:25:56.260
And we should also grieve today

00:25:56.260 --> 00:25:59.720
for those who have suffered similar injustices

00:25:59.720 --> 00:26:02.980
and who may now have to revisit their own experiences

00:26:02.980 --> 00:26:04.080
as they hear this news.

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:06.240
We grieve those losses.

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:10.840
There's probably a whole range of emotions that we feel with this.

00:26:11.700 --> 00:26:20.180
Anger, fear, sadness, anxiety, mistrust, worry, stress.

00:26:21.480 --> 00:26:24.520
These past few weeks have been probably the most stressful time in my life.

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:30.060
And as we process all of those, just an encouragement I want to give to all of us

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:36.080
is that it's so important that we process our feelings in light of the gospel in community

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:42.100
with one another. And I want to encourage all of us in the coming days and weeks to just be kind

00:26:42.100 --> 00:26:47.260
and patient with each other. Sometimes the best things we can do are simply sit with each other

00:26:47.260 --> 00:26:53.100
if we're going through things, listen to what someone is sharing, their experiences, pray for

00:26:53.100 --> 00:26:58.020
one another, and just patiently go through the process of going through all of these different

00:26:58.020 --> 00:27:05.720
feelings that we may have. Now, as we call sin, sin, and even process all of our reactions and grief

00:27:05.720 --> 00:27:07.200
and emotions that come with that.

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:11.020
When this has to do with someone who is a Christian leader,

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:14.920
we can't simply kind of push it to the side and move on.

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:16.540
I think it's very important

00:27:16.540 --> 00:27:18.720
that we also bring darkness into the light.

00:27:20.120 --> 00:27:21.820
When something like this happens specifically

00:27:21.820 --> 00:27:23.980
with a pastor or elder of the church,

00:27:24.100 --> 00:27:26.760
we can't just deal with it privately and quietly.

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:29.580
The Bible very clearly instructs us on this.

00:27:29.740 --> 00:27:31.600
1 Timothy 5, 19 to 20 says,

00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:33.680
do not admit a charge against an elder

00:27:33.680 --> 00:27:36.440
except on the evidence of two or three witnesses.

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:38.860
As for those who persist in sin,

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:40.620
rebuke them in the presence of all

00:27:40.620 --> 00:27:42.680
so that the rest may stand in fear.

00:27:43.720 --> 00:27:46.500
One thing I want to make really clear to you as well

00:27:46.500 --> 00:27:48.140
here within our church family

00:27:48.140 --> 00:27:51.280
is that from the moment we first heard of Seth's misconduct,

00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:53.940
our elder team has been united in our intention

00:27:53.940 --> 00:27:55.500
to bring this into the light.

00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:58.040
It is the right biblical thing to do

00:27:58.040 --> 00:28:00.800
and we never questioned or debated that for a moment.

00:28:01.020 --> 00:28:03.600
It has always been what we planned to do.

00:28:03.680 --> 00:28:09.420
but some of us here I don't know how we think about these things what background you might be

00:28:09.420 --> 00:28:14.420
coming from how you think about leadership in the church maybe some of us here today wonder why in

00:28:14.420 --> 00:28:20.160
this case it should be so very public why should we announce this to the world why should this even

00:28:20.160 --> 00:28:27.400
we even post this on our church's website well I'll explain it this way there are have been many

00:28:27.400 --> 00:28:33.280
leaders not only Seth but others as well who want a big platform who want a lot of influence

00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:35.680
a big audience to listen to them

00:28:35.680 --> 00:28:36.840
and be under their leadership.

00:28:37.380 --> 00:28:39.100
But when misconduct occurs,

00:28:39.100 --> 00:28:41.840
then want to keep everything quiet and undercover.

00:28:42.780 --> 00:28:44.900
And this is what this passage is trying to tell us,

00:28:44.940 --> 00:28:46.780
is that the scope of public correction

00:28:46.780 --> 00:28:49.740
should match the scope of that person's public ministry.

00:28:50.940 --> 00:28:52.700
And this is why we cannot just,

00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:54.020
even if it would be more comfortable,

00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:56.380
more convenient for us as a church here,

00:28:56.580 --> 00:28:58.860
this is why we don't feel like we can just move on

00:28:58.860 --> 00:28:59.700
as if nothing happened,

00:28:59.760 --> 00:29:03.220
that we must bring what was in darkness into the light.

00:29:04.280 --> 00:29:06.540
What we see in the scripture and in this passage

00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:08.940
is that there are a couple of purposes for doing this.

00:29:09.460 --> 00:29:10.700
The first purpose of doing this

00:29:10.700 --> 00:29:12.460
is to call that person to repentance

00:29:12.460 --> 00:29:14.560
and to make it unavoidable

00:29:14.560 --> 00:29:17.500
to have to deal with what they have done.

00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:20.300
This is what we hope for in this situation.

00:29:21.500 --> 00:29:22.420
One thing I want to share,

00:29:22.540 --> 00:29:25.520
which I think is a scandalous truth of the gospel

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:27.400
that people may have different feelings about,

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:28.440
but it is true,

00:29:28.700 --> 00:29:30.600
is that real repentance and restoration

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:33.440
are available to even the worst of sinners.

00:29:34.420 --> 00:29:36.220
That is true and real.

00:29:36.940 --> 00:29:38.620
One thing I want to make clear with that

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:39.940
and make sure it's very clear

00:29:39.940 --> 00:29:41.620
is that in cases like this,

00:29:42.080 --> 00:29:45.720
restoration is restoration to fellowship with the church,

00:29:45.880 --> 00:29:47.980
not restoration to a pastoral role.

00:29:48.860 --> 00:29:50.640
That needs to be very clear.

00:29:51.580 --> 00:29:53.500
And what would true repentance look like

00:29:53.500 --> 00:29:54.940
in a situation like this?

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:57.420
True repentance would look like

00:29:57.420 --> 00:30:00.080
a full and honest public confession.

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:06.700
It would look like a genuine apology and amends to those who have been hurt.

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:13.160
It would look like submitting to oversight by those who can give real accountability and guidance in the situation

00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:17.760
and humble and transparent character change over a long period of time.

00:30:18.360 --> 00:30:21.020
That's what real repentance would look like.

00:30:21.020 --> 00:30:29.860
I should also make clear that a leader who has fallen into sin cannot and should not try to dictate what his own accountability or restoration should look like.

00:30:30.600 --> 00:30:33.200
That should be done under the supervision of trusted leaders

00:30:33.200 --> 00:30:36.740
who will hold that person accountable and guide that restoration process.

00:30:37.420 --> 00:30:40.840
This is one of the purposes, to call that person to repentance.

00:30:41.700 --> 00:30:45.240
But a second purpose, it says clearly in this passage,

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:47.740
so that the rest may stand in fear.

00:30:48.760 --> 00:30:51.420
So that the rest may stand in fear.

00:30:52.160 --> 00:30:53.020
Who are the rest?

00:30:53.880 --> 00:30:56.000
Well, other elders, other pastors.

00:30:57.100 --> 00:30:58.940
But I would also say all of us.

00:30:58.940 --> 00:31:01.980
so that all of us should stand in fear.

00:31:03.180 --> 00:31:06.380
Acknowledging, as we see, God is a holy God.

00:31:06.460 --> 00:31:07.320
In him is light.

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:08.960
There's no darkness at all.

00:31:09.280 --> 00:31:11.280
That we should take sin seriously.

00:31:12.360 --> 00:31:13.740
And one thing I think we should recognize

00:31:13.740 --> 00:31:15.540
is that the other side of this coin,

00:31:15.660 --> 00:31:17.280
of what tends to happen a lot,

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:19.900
is when the church doesn't take sin seriously,

00:31:20.040 --> 00:31:22.220
we end up losing the fear of God altogether.

00:31:23.080 --> 00:31:25.620
We start making light of God's grace.

00:31:26.100 --> 00:31:28.180
We start making light of the blood of Christ

00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:29.800
that was shed for us.

00:31:30.540 --> 00:31:32.300
We start making light of the offenses,

00:31:32.520 --> 00:31:33.460
not only against other people,

00:31:33.540 --> 00:31:34.620
but against God himself.

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:37.820
And when this is brought into the light,

00:31:37.900 --> 00:31:40.320
it says we stand in the fear of God

00:31:40.320 --> 00:31:43.100
and we cannot call sin anything else but what it is.

00:31:45.920 --> 00:31:49.000
That's why as we bring that darkness into the light,

00:31:49.620 --> 00:31:51.600
this is an opportunity for us as well

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:52.700
to examine ourselves.

00:31:55.120 --> 00:31:57.340
I want to acknowledge that the majority of you here today

00:31:57.340 --> 00:32:00.360
had absolutely nothing to do with any of this

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:02.600
and don't even know the people involved in it.

00:32:03.240 --> 00:32:06.940
But this is still an opportunity for us to reflect and examine ourselves

00:32:06.940 --> 00:32:11.380
both as a church community and as individual followers of Jesus.

00:32:12.460 --> 00:32:16.780
Starting out, I think it's an opportunity examining ourselves as a church community.

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:21.820
For our elder team especially, there's already been a great deal of evaluation and reflection

00:32:21.820 --> 00:32:24.020
about what all of this means for us.

00:32:24.680 --> 00:32:27.200
And we've been asking ourselves important questions like,

00:32:27.200 --> 00:32:29.820
just how do we live in the truth of the gospel together?

00:32:30.360 --> 00:32:33.360
How do we practice accountability in leadership?

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:36.960
How do we make it safe for people to raise concerns when they come up?

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:40.720
Are we leading by biblical teachings or by personal preferences?

00:32:41.640 --> 00:32:46.360
Are we leading with transparency and integrity or secrecy and hypocrisy?

00:32:47.040 --> 00:32:49.900
We've had to evaluate and ask ourselves all these questions.

00:32:50.180 --> 00:32:52.740
And I do want to share that over recent years,

00:32:53.360 --> 00:32:55.640
we've made a number of changes already in our church

00:32:55.640 --> 00:32:57.360
to move in a healthy biblical direction

00:32:57.360 --> 00:33:02.740
direction that we knew would be pleasing to God and healthy for our church. Many of you already

00:33:02.740 --> 00:33:07.900
know that we've done things like created a direct pathway to report concerns to the elders through

00:33:07.900 --> 00:33:14.060
our website anonymously, if desired. That we've also done things like transition from a single

00:33:14.060 --> 00:33:20.000
authority leadership model, which would be called like an Episcopalian governance, to an elder team

00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:25.660
led model, where I am not the sole decision maker for our church and nobody is. Where our elder team

00:33:25.660 --> 00:33:31.440
leads together and I don't hold any more authority than them or them than me. Because we want to be

00:33:31.440 --> 00:33:37.180
in accountability to one another, not with one person calling all the shots. Take practical steps

00:33:37.180 --> 00:33:41.600
like putting security cameras up around our facilities to make sure that things are done

00:33:41.600 --> 00:33:46.060
in the light. And even with accountability, things like setting clear expectations

00:33:46.060 --> 00:33:51.500
for our leaders and among our pastors having every other week accountability times with each other

00:33:51.500 --> 00:33:54.340
to make sure we're in healthy relationship with God.

00:33:54.560 --> 00:33:57.440
We're doing all of these things in recent months.

00:33:57.980 --> 00:34:01.140
Our focus has really been on getting to this point here today,

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:03.320
but we're planning to take further steps

00:34:03.320 --> 00:34:05.260
to live with integrity and transparency

00:34:05.260 --> 00:34:06.380
in light of the gospel,

00:34:06.380 --> 00:34:08.940
and we commit that to you as a church.

00:34:10.320 --> 00:34:12.660
One thing that I want to make very clear

00:34:12.660 --> 00:34:15.120
as a clear expectation for our church

00:34:15.120 --> 00:34:18.380
is that sin should never be covered up in our church family.

00:34:18.380 --> 00:34:25.760
sin should never be covered up in our church family even if that's myself or one of the other

00:34:25.760 --> 00:34:32.740
pastors or the elders if there's a concern it should be brought up and shared and we encourage

00:34:32.740 --> 00:34:38.720
that and want that to be the culture of our church community so we examine that what does that mean

00:34:38.720 --> 00:34:44.980
for us as a church family but also as I mentioned examining ourselves means looking at ourselves as

00:34:44.980 --> 00:34:49.820
followers of Jesus. You know, this doesn't mean that any of you had anything to do with what's

00:34:49.820 --> 00:34:56.600
happened, but it's an opportunity to reflect, to ask ourselves questions like, do I open myself

00:34:56.600 --> 00:35:05.040
up to accountability, or do I avoid accountability? Do I hold others accountable, or do I play it safe

00:35:05.040 --> 00:35:09.680
and take the more comfortable route of not saying anything, even when I see something

00:35:09.680 --> 00:35:17.060
concerning. Pastor Herschel York says, sin is always harmful. The cruelest thing you can do

00:35:17.060 --> 00:35:22.940
is leave someone in sin. The kindest thing you can do is do everything possible to get them

00:35:22.940 --> 00:35:29.540
out of sin. I think this is where I won't read the verses, but in Matthew 18, you see that Jesus

00:35:29.540 --> 00:35:34.720
describes how we should hold each other accountable in the church, bringing up concerns with one

00:35:34.720 --> 00:35:39.480
another. If need, bringing along other witnesses. If need, even confronting it with the church as a

00:35:39.480 --> 00:35:46.360
whole. These are things Jesus brought up because he knew there would be sin in the church. He knew

00:35:46.360 --> 00:35:52.160
people would fall short. He knew that things would happen that would cause divisions in our

00:35:52.160 --> 00:35:57.040
fellowship or that would cause damage to our witness or so many other things. He knew that

00:35:57.040 --> 00:36:03.420
would happen. I will also make sure it's clear here. If this is in cases of serious sin, of things

00:36:03.420 --> 00:36:07.440
and sexual misconduct like we've talked about today, something where you're fearful about

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:11.440
what's going to happen. You should not feel that you need to bring up those things directly with

00:36:11.440 --> 00:36:17.100
an individual first. You can immediately ask others for help, and you should do that. But we

00:36:17.100 --> 00:36:22.240
should learn to practice these teachings of Jesus together as the family of the church.

00:36:23.580 --> 00:36:29.120
We should also ask ourselves questions like, okay, I'm willing to call somebody else's sin,

00:36:29.460 --> 00:36:38.160
sin, but am I willing to call my own sin, sin? I realize for many of us, it's easy to point out

00:36:38.160 --> 00:36:44.860
the flaws of others. It's easy to say that somebody else has done something wrong, but when we look at

00:36:44.860 --> 00:36:50.180
ourselves, we say, oh, well, I just made a little mistake, or oh, I didn't really mean to do that.

00:36:50.380 --> 00:36:56.760
We kind of give ourselves a whole lot of permission to do whatever we want to do. Do I call my sin,

00:36:57.020 --> 00:37:02.220
sin, and do I recognize that every single one of us actually has a sin problem that needs to be

00:37:02.220 --> 00:37:06.080
dealt with? I shared this quote, I think, a few months ago with our church, but I'll share it

00:37:06.080 --> 00:37:11.360
again, from Alexander Solzhenitsyn. He wrote, gradually it was disclosed to me that the line

00:37:11.360 --> 00:37:16.640
separating good and evil passes not through states, nor between classes, nor between political

00:37:16.640 --> 00:37:23.080
parties, but right through every human heart and through all human hearts. I think in a situation

00:37:23.080 --> 00:37:30.980
like this, it's tempting to think, oh, well, those people sin. That kind of person sins. But we need

00:37:30.980 --> 00:37:38.680
to recognize all of us sin. All of us stand in need of the grace of God. And so when we think

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:44.700
about this and evaluate ourselves, are we willing to ask for forgiveness and also even forgive

00:37:44.700 --> 00:37:52.920
others? Do I live in the light, in fellowship with God? Am I spiritually healthy? One thing I've

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:59.020
realized through all of this is that we can and should do everything we possibly can to practice

00:37:59.020 --> 00:38:04.960
accountability, to put boundaries and safeguards in place, to have good policies around what we do

00:38:04.960 --> 00:38:10.380
as a church. We should do all of those things, but if someone is spiritually unhealthy and far

00:38:10.380 --> 00:38:17.280
from God and wants to sin, they will probably find a way to be able to do it. I really believe

00:38:17.280 --> 00:38:22.740
on top of all those things that we can and should do, the best protection against serious sin and

00:38:22.740 --> 00:38:28.580
temptation is to be spiritually and relationally healthy. That's what a lot of it comes down to.

00:38:29.020 --> 00:38:34.460
And so at the end here, what I would want to encourage us to do mostly is to turn to Jesus.

00:38:35.620 --> 00:38:37.920
I'll read verses 7 and 9 for us again.

00:38:38.680 --> 00:38:38.820
It says,

00:38:38.820 --> 00:38:43.460
But if we walk in the light as he is in the light, we have fellowship with one another,

00:38:43.760 --> 00:38:46.720
and the blood of Jesus his Son cleanses us from all sin.

00:38:47.260 --> 00:38:53.620
If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness.

00:38:54.620 --> 00:38:57.700
This is an invitation for every single one of us today

00:38:57.700 --> 00:39:01.680
to turn to Jesus, recognizing our need for him,

00:39:01.900 --> 00:39:04.340
recognizing that we ourselves fall short,

00:39:04.760 --> 00:39:06.640
recognizing that there is much to grieve

00:39:06.640 --> 00:39:07.640
over what has happened,

00:39:08.120 --> 00:39:10.120
recognizing that it is Christ alone

00:39:10.120 --> 00:39:13.060
who can actually make a path forward for us.

00:39:13.280 --> 00:39:14.980
Why do we turn to Jesus?

00:39:15.140 --> 00:39:16.760
Why should we turn to Jesus?

00:39:16.760 --> 00:39:19.000
Well, first, because as we've talked about today,

00:39:19.160 --> 00:39:22.440
he is light, and in him there is no darkness at all.

00:39:22.440 --> 00:39:25.640
and we can trust him to actually shepherd us

00:39:25.640 --> 00:39:28.160
and lead us and guide us through

00:39:28.160 --> 00:39:30.020
everything that we need to go through.

00:39:30.420 --> 00:39:32.860
But we also turn to Jesus most importantly

00:39:32.860 --> 00:39:35.860
because his blood cleanses our sins.

00:39:36.580 --> 00:39:39.900
This is the hope that all of us can have in Christ

00:39:39.900 --> 00:39:43.220
because we all sin and fall short of the glory of God,

00:39:43.480 --> 00:39:45.460
sometimes in ways that seem big to us,

00:39:45.740 --> 00:39:47.920
sometimes in ways that seem small to us.

00:39:48.040 --> 00:39:49.560
But in every one of those ways,

00:39:49.560 --> 00:39:56.200
we cannot save ourselves or pay the penalty for our own sin, we must bring that to Jesus who died

00:39:56.200 --> 00:40:01.460
on the cross so that we can be forgiven and restored to relationship with God and one another.

00:40:01.660 --> 00:40:07.220
This is the only path forward. And by the way, I should also mention that in the cross of Jesus

00:40:07.220 --> 00:40:13.980
Christ, we see both the forgiveness and justice of God. For some of us, even in these situations,

00:40:13.980 --> 00:40:19.180
we wonder, God, where is justice? Where will things be made right? And there's so much of

00:40:19.180 --> 00:40:24.120
that that we can't answer, we can't know, we don't know what God's plans are. But one thing I am

00:40:24.120 --> 00:40:30.260
absolutely certain of is that the same Savior who bled and died on the cross and was buried and rose

00:40:30.260 --> 00:40:36.540
again, that he will return to bring justice and righteousness on the earth. And there is not a

00:40:36.540 --> 00:40:41.040
single sin that will not either be atoned for with the blood of Jesus Christ or will not have to

00:40:41.040 --> 00:40:47.320
stand before the judgment of God. We can know that and stand firm in that and at the same time come

00:40:47.320 --> 00:40:54.060
to God and say, God, we've received your forgiveness. Now, an encouragement I want to

00:40:54.060 --> 00:41:00.080
give to all of us is we must also make the choice to forgive because we have been forgiven.

00:41:01.540 --> 00:41:07.080
I want to draw a distinction again that forgiveness is not the same as permissiveness about sin.

00:41:08.420 --> 00:41:13.720
It does not mean that we just say, oh, that didn't matter, that didn't hurt, that didn't do any damage,

00:41:13.720 --> 00:41:20.160
whatever, it's okay. Forgiveness is not permissiveness. It is not glossing over. But

00:41:20.160 --> 00:41:25.600
what forgiveness is and why it's so important is that forgiveness, forgiving others who have sinned

00:41:25.600 --> 00:41:30.640
against us, protects us from the seeds of hatred and bitterness that can come and take root in our

00:41:30.640 --> 00:41:38.580
hearts and start to bear fruit. And holding on to anger, holding on to hatred, holding on to

00:41:38.580 --> 00:41:43.620
accusation, holding on to just feelings of judgment within ourselves, holding on to those things,

00:41:43.720 --> 00:41:46.560
does not do good for us or for anybody else.

00:41:47.420 --> 00:41:50.320
And when Christ died on that cross to forgive us of our sins,

00:41:50.420 --> 00:41:55.520
he did it also to set us free from hatred, bitterness, anger,

00:41:55.720 --> 00:41:56.780
and all the rest of it.

00:41:56.840 --> 00:41:58.100
Say we can be free from that,

00:41:58.140 --> 00:42:01.560
and we can release those things to Jesus at the foot of the cross.

00:42:03.220 --> 00:42:05.040
So this is why we turn to Jesus.

00:42:05.700 --> 00:42:09.520
And lastly, I will mention we turn to Jesus because he is building his church.

00:42:10.920 --> 00:42:12.840
Matthew 16, 18, Jesus said,

00:42:12.840 --> 00:42:18.020
I tell you, you are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church, and the gates of hell shall not

00:42:18.020 --> 00:42:26.400
prevail against it. This tells us that Jesus is the one who is loving, protecting, and building

00:42:26.400 --> 00:42:32.320
his church. And something, I'm not sure we would think about it this way, but something I want all

00:42:32.320 --> 00:42:38.740
of us to know is even this, what we're sharing today, what we're bringing to the light today,

00:42:38.740 --> 00:42:41.700
what we're having to deal with and process today,

00:42:41.880 --> 00:42:45.060
this is part of Jesus building his church.

00:42:45.960 --> 00:42:49.700
This is part of Jesus purifying his bride.

00:42:50.560 --> 00:42:53.660
This is part of Jesus setting us free,

00:42:53.980 --> 00:42:56.380
calling us to live in integrity and holiness.

00:42:56.560 --> 00:42:58.500
This is part of Jesus saying,

00:42:58.800 --> 00:43:01.860
you, my people, will be different than the world around you.

00:43:02.820 --> 00:43:05.480
This is Jesus building his church.

00:43:05.480 --> 00:43:08.780
and he will lead us through all of this.

00:43:09.380 --> 00:43:11.140
Through all of this, he will shape us

00:43:11.140 --> 00:43:12.420
to be more like himself.

00:43:12.960 --> 00:43:14.400
Through this, he will empower us

00:43:14.400 --> 00:43:16.120
to live out his calling together

00:43:16.120 --> 00:43:19.020
and through all of this, he is inviting us,

00:43:19.740 --> 00:43:20.740
saying, come to me.

00:43:21.320 --> 00:43:22.760
Even examine yourselves.

00:43:23.040 --> 00:43:24.300
Examine yourselves together.

00:43:24.560 --> 00:43:26.220
Examine yourselves personally.

00:43:26.700 --> 00:43:29.240
Turn to Jesus and find there hope

00:43:29.240 --> 00:43:31.160
and salvation and forgiveness

00:43:31.160 --> 00:43:33.060
and life and freedom.

00:43:33.300 --> 00:43:35.020
Come and find that in me.

00:43:35.480 --> 00:43:40.780
and that's why today, even as we process through all of this, I felt it would be a very appropriate

00:43:40.780 --> 00:43:46.080
way, as I close out today's message, to actually end this time by taking communion together.

00:43:47.180 --> 00:43:52.260
And we do this because when we come to the communion table, we're reminded each time

00:43:52.260 --> 00:44:00.240
that we cannot save ourselves. We're reminded that we cannot earn our own forgiveness or redemption.

00:44:01.220 --> 00:44:03.720
We are reminded of the price that was paid on the cross

00:44:03.720 --> 00:44:06.340
for our salvation and forgiveness of sin.

00:44:07.200 --> 00:44:08.660
We're reminded that there is hope,

00:44:09.000 --> 00:44:11.180
even in the face of the worst sin,

00:44:11.880 --> 00:44:13.760
that there is hope in the face of it

00:44:13.760 --> 00:44:16.260
because of what it is that Christ has done for us.

00:44:17.140 --> 00:44:19.620
And today, the encouragement I want to give to all of us

00:44:19.620 --> 00:44:21.120
that I want to invite all of us into

00:44:21.120 --> 00:44:23.340
is that as we spend this time today

00:44:23.340 --> 00:44:24.860
to take communion together,

00:44:25.440 --> 00:44:27.540
that we come to the Lord with all the things

00:44:27.540 --> 00:44:28.680
that we are going through,

00:44:28.680 --> 00:44:30.680
whether that is anger

00:44:30.680 --> 00:44:33.060
whether that is grieving

00:44:33.060 --> 00:44:35.420
whether that is uncertainty

00:44:35.420 --> 00:44:37.400
whether that is fear

00:44:37.400 --> 00:44:40.220
whether that is maybe guilt or shame

00:44:40.220 --> 00:44:41.160
for different reasons

00:44:41.160 --> 00:44:42.040
whatever that is

00:44:42.040 --> 00:44:43.060
to come to the Lord

00:44:43.060 --> 00:44:45.180
and say Lord this is where we meet you

00:44:45.180 --> 00:44:47.800
this is where we receive grace and forgiveness

00:44:47.800 --> 00:44:49.800
that we so desperately need

00:44:49.800 --> 00:44:52.100
can I invite us all to bow our heads together

00:44:52.100 --> 00:44:54.640
as we spend some time responding to the Lord this morning

00:44:54.640 --> 00:45:20.820
as we bow our heads here before the Lord

00:45:20.820 --> 00:45:27.160
I feel for many of us and for us together

00:45:27.160 --> 00:45:29.180
this is a kind

00:45:29.180 --> 00:45:30.120
of decision point

00:45:30.120 --> 00:45:36.100
when we're confronted with things like this

00:45:36.100 --> 00:45:37.820
which direction will we turn

00:45:37.820 --> 00:45:44.900
again do we try to avoid the reality

00:45:44.900 --> 00:45:46.980
of sin or do we call it

00:45:46.980 --> 00:45:47.720
for what it is

00:45:47.720 --> 00:45:52.740
will we try to hide in darkness

00:45:52.740 --> 00:45:55.340
or will we bring things into the light

00:45:55.340 --> 00:46:02.160
will we examine ourselves

00:46:02.160 --> 00:46:04.340
or will we pretend

00:46:04.340 --> 00:46:06.060
as if nothing really happened

00:46:06.060 --> 00:46:09.760
will we turn to Jesus

00:46:09.760 --> 00:46:15.560
or will we turn toward bitterness, hatred,

00:46:17.560 --> 00:46:20.340
condemnation of others or even condemnation of ourselves?

00:46:23.580 --> 00:46:26.120
I want to give us some time to prepare to take communion today.

00:46:27.360 --> 00:46:30.580
And as I give us a couple minutes while we prepare the communion table,

00:46:30.580 --> 00:46:34.520
I want to encourage you to use this time to come and meet with God personally.

00:46:37.230 --> 00:46:39.490
I want to give some guidance around this because it's important.

00:46:40.450 --> 00:46:43.650
The Bible tells us not to come to the communion table in an unworthy manner.

00:46:44.650 --> 00:46:47.350
For those of us here who are followers of Jesus Christ,

00:46:47.870 --> 00:46:50.210
what that means is that we do not come

00:46:50.210 --> 00:46:52.610
and frivolously participate in communion,

00:46:53.890 --> 00:46:55.570
eating and drinking the bread and the cup,

00:46:56.230 --> 00:46:58.190
but we come in humility,

00:46:59.290 --> 00:47:01.790
saying, Lord, I am one of those people

00:47:01.790 --> 00:47:03.150
that could not save myself.

00:47:03.910 --> 00:47:06.410
I am one of those people that needs forgiveness,

00:47:07.630 --> 00:47:10.090
that needs healing, that needs freedom.

00:47:10.750 --> 00:47:13.270
I am one of those people that have sinned and fallen short.

00:47:13.950 --> 00:47:17.550
And I would encourage all of us here who are followers of Jesus to use these moments

00:47:17.550 --> 00:47:21.650
to confess anything that we need to to the Lord.

00:47:23.660 --> 00:47:27.920
Whether that is sin, whether that is even just different feelings and responses we're having today.

00:47:28.800 --> 00:47:31.500
To come and bring that before the Lord, even in our grief.

00:47:32.500 --> 00:47:34.900
And say, Lord, this is why I need you today.

00:47:35.820 --> 00:47:37.320
I would encourage us to do that.

00:47:38.340 --> 00:47:41.860
Also, there may be some of you here today who aren't yet followers of Jesus Christ.

00:47:42.400 --> 00:47:43.300
We're so glad you're here.

00:47:46.080 --> 00:47:48.180
One thing I want to make clear is that the Bible tells us

00:47:48.180 --> 00:47:50.700
that participating in communion is only for those people

00:47:50.700 --> 00:47:52.420
who do believe in Jesus,

00:47:52.880 --> 00:47:55.380
who identify with him in his death and his resurrection,

00:47:55.560 --> 00:47:58.160
who believe that his blood forgives us for our sins.

00:47:59.040 --> 00:48:01.500
So if you're not a person who believes those things yet,

00:48:01.580 --> 00:48:03.920
please just let the communion elements pass by you

00:48:03.920 --> 00:48:05.420
as they come around the room later.

00:48:06.320 --> 00:48:07.860
I also want to give this invitation

00:48:07.860 --> 00:48:11.440
because even in something like you heard today,

00:48:11.760 --> 00:48:13.700
you may come to a realization

00:48:13.700 --> 00:48:16.380
that you also could not save yourself.

00:48:17.660 --> 00:48:21.160
A realization that no other person is going to save you either.

00:48:21.560 --> 00:48:27.000
No church leader, no ability of somebody else,

00:48:27.060 --> 00:48:28.040
no ability of your own.

00:48:28.840 --> 00:48:31.220
All people fall short, including you.

00:48:32.280 --> 00:48:34.800
And maybe you realize today that you also need forgiveness.

00:48:36.280 --> 00:48:39.620
You also need eternal life that can only come from faith in Jesus.

00:48:39.860 --> 00:48:41.840
If you want to place your faith in Jesus today,

00:48:41.840 --> 00:48:43.920
we invite you to tell him

00:48:43.920 --> 00:48:45.900
that you want to believe and trust that he died on the

00:48:45.900 --> 00:48:47.940
cross for you. Tell

00:48:47.940 --> 00:48:50.140
him that you want to follow him faithfully

00:48:50.140 --> 00:48:51.900
knowing that you will fall short again and

